# HAS A BUILD GOT SLOWER, IS A JOB NEAR ITS CEILING, IS MAIN WAITING FOR A RUNNER -- asked of the build timings every
# workflow pushes as its last job (scripts/ci/ci_timings.py), so nobody first hears of a slowdown when a job hits its
# `timeout-minutes` and goes red.
#
# WHY. 4b2e9cb04 made the convergence `takes` rows slower -- Poland 19-33 min to past its 55-minute ceiling, the UK
# 34-42 to past it, the US 27-35 to 46.5 -- and the first anyone heard was run 1641 timing out. Main's own end to end
# went from 28 to 42 minutes over a few days, and a recording's Germany leg from 18 to 125, with no word either. The
# "CI — build timings" dashboard drew all of it; nobody was looking.
#
# THE SERIES (ci_timings.py's header), one sample per run, every one stamped at the RUN'S START and pushed when the run
# ends -- hours later for a convergence leg. So nothing here reads a raw selector (its 5-minute lookback would never see
# a sample): every read is a range function over a window wider than the longest run.
#
#   kinowo_ci_job_seconds{workflow,ref,ci_job,conclusion,runner}  a job's wall clock
#   kinowo_ci_job_queue_seconds{...as job_seconds}            created -> started (dashboard only, no rule reads it)
#   kinowo_ci_step_seconds{workflow,ref,ci_job,step,runner}   a step's
#   kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow,ref,runner,measure,conclusion}  `wall` (first job start to last job end), `runner_wait`
#   kinowo_ci_job_timeout_seconds{workflow,ref,ci_job,runner} the job's own `timeout-minutes`
#   kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow,ref,runner}                 the run's index, the number in its URL
#
# `runner` is `ci-runner` (self-hosted), `hosted` or `unknown` on a job's series; on the run-level ones (run_seconds,
# run_number) it is the whole run's: `hosted`, `ci-runner`, `mixed` or `unknown`. LIKE WITH LIKE: a job or run that moves
# runner starts a new series, with its own baseline (a self-hosted runner is not judged against GitHub's); every
# pairing below (`> baseline`, `/ timeout`) matches on all labels, so it pairs a series with its own runner kind and
# ref, and the descriptions look the baseline and the runs up by that runner too. Samples before 2026-11-06 have no
# `runner`.
#
# `ref` is "main" or "branch" (nothing else; ci_timings.py ref_of). No commit SHA is a label (one series per run); the run number is a sample VALUE, and the
# regression alerts read it to name the runs a regression started between: the last run at baseline and the first slow
# one after it. Their commits are the range.
#
# THE BASELINE is each series' own: the median of its SUCCESSFUL main runs from 9 to 2 days ago, given at least three.
# Successful only: a failed or cancelled job stopped early, and its short time would drag the median down. Ending two
# days back keeps the runs being judged out of the baseline that judges them. A cost that stays becomes this baseline
# once it is half of the week; until then the REFERENCE below has already learned it.
#
# A REGRESSION is the MEDIAN of the last two days' successful runs over baseline + max(40%, 5 minutes): the ratio
# because legs vary +-25% run to run (Poland's takes 19-33 around 26), the floor so a 40-second job taking a minute is
# not news, and the median of two days, given two runs, so that it says what the runs now take rather than what the
# latest one did. Until 2026-11-15 it read the LATEST run and any one other over the threshold, or one run over twice
# the baseline: 321 episodes in the week to 2026-11-15, nearly all of them one run in a mix -- the drift rows, whose
# replay is read from the store or run (7 or 20 minutes, both normal), Tool self-tests' ci-runner queue (3 minutes,
# 12-40 when the runners are busy), Identity lean vs whole skipping or not (0 or 30). A workflow running twenty times a
# day always has two such runs in two days. The median moves only when half the runs moved, which is what a commit
# does, and it moved for the store filling (2026-11-12: drift rows 15 -> 25 minutes). A nightly job has two runs in the
# window: one of them over twice the baseline (Germany's leg 18 -> 125) still lifts their median over the threshold.
# One slow run among many is a busy runner, not a regression; CiJobNearTimeout is the alert for a run near its ceiling.
# Against the motivating runs: Main 42 over 28 fires once half its runs take it (threshold 39.2), US takes 46.5 over a
# 31 median (43.4) likewise, Poland's 33 over 26 does not (36.4).
#
# EACH SERIES' WINDOW FOLLOWS ITS OWN CADENCE (2026-11-16). The windows above need three runs in the week and two in
# the last two days, which a weekly or dispatch-only workflow (a weekly bump, Verify scrape recording on a quiet week)
# never has: it could never alert. So the baseline is chosen by the series' observed spacing: the two-day tier
# (`window="2d"`) where the week before the last two days holds three runs, else the two-week tier (`window="14d"`):
# the median of the six weeks before the last two weeks, given three runs, judged by the median of the last two
# weeks', given two (`or ignoring (window)` keeps one tier per series; retention is 90 days). The alert pairs a
# recent median with the baseline of its own tier, so it carries `window`, and its description reads that tier's
# words and looks the runs up over its span (9 days at a 5-minute step, 56 days at an hourly one).
#
# A SHIFT IS REPORTED FOR A DAY, THEN LEARNED (2026-11-18). A cost that stays -- a slowdown nobody will undo, a
# deliberate change (runner caches, a test shard split, a cpuset) -- reached the baseline only once it was half of
# the baseline's window: it was reported for about five days on the two-day tier and weeks on the two-week one, in
# every daily digest. On 2026-11-17 that was 34 step and 36 job alerts, nearly all for changes made on purpose. So a
# series is judged against its REFERENCE: the baseline, or, when its recent median was ALREADY over the threshold a
# day ago, that recent median of a day ago -- the level it moved to. A regression fires on the day it shows (held 6 h
# past, so it is in one digest) and is quiet the next; a further rise on top of the new level (40% / 5 minutes over
# it) fires again; once the baseline has caught up, the reference is the baseline again. A known change needs no
# reset by hand: the day after it shows, it is the level. (A nightly jump whose first median is half old, half new,
# is reported for two days: the day-old median it learns is the mix.)
#
# A SERIES THAT STOPPED IS NOT JUDGED. A label change (`runner`, 2026-11-06; a renamed job or step) ends a series;
# with no runs in the last two days it falls to the two-week tier, whose window still holds its final runs, judged
# against the six weeks before: of the 36 regression alerts firing in the day to 2026-11-18, 27 were series with no
# run in 8 days. The two-week tier needs a run in the last 8 days (a weekly workflow always has one).
#
# THE DESCRIPTIONS' RUN LOOKUPS READ ONLY THEIR OWN SERIES' THRESHOLD. They compared each run with every series'
# threshold (`ci:step_seconds:regression_threshold @ end()`, unfiltered) over a 56-day subquery, once per firing alert
# per evaluation: on 2026-11-18 the group took 420 s of its 300 s interval, 370 of them CiStepDurationRegressed's
# thirteen descriptions, so evaluations were skipped and the recordings had gaps. One such lookup took 3.9 s, scoped 0.8.
groups:
  - name: ci-build-times
    # Every rule reads days of a sparse series; nothing here needs to be fresher than a run is long.
    interval: 5m
    rules:
      # job: the two-day tier where the week before holds three runs, else the two-week tier (`or ignoring (window)`).
      - record: ci:job_seconds:baseline
        expr: >-
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[7d] offset 2d)
            and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[7d] offset 2d) >= 3,
            "window", "2d", "", "")
          or ignoring (window)
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[42d] offset 14d)
            and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[42d] offset 14d) >= 3,
            "window", "14d", "", "")
      - record: ci:job_seconds:regression_threshold
        expr: ci:job_seconds:baseline + clamp_min(0.4 * ci:job_seconds:baseline, 300)
      # the median of each tier's window, given two runs in it; the alert pairs it with the baseline of its own tier
      - record: ci:job_seconds:recent_median
        expr: >-
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[2d]) and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[2d]) >= 2,
            "window", "2d", "", "")
          or
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[14d]) and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[14d]) >= 2
            and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[8d]) >= 1,
            "window", "14d", "", "")
      # the reference it is judged by: the baseline, or the level it was already past its threshold at a day ago
      - record: ci:job_seconds:reference
        expr: >-
          (ci:job_seconds:recent_median offset 1d > ci:job_seconds:regression_threshold offset 1d)
          or ignoring (window) ci:job_seconds:baseline
      - record: ci:job_seconds:reference_threshold
        expr: ci:job_seconds:reference + clamp_min(0.4 * ci:job_seconds:reference, 300)

      # step: the two-day tier where the week before holds three runs, else the two-week tier (`or ignoring (window)`).
      - record: ci:step_seconds:baseline
        expr: >-
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[7d] offset 2d)
            and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[7d] offset 2d) >= 3,
            "window", "2d", "", "")
          or ignoring (window)
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[42d] offset 14d)
            and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[42d] offset 14d) >= 3,
            "window", "14d", "", "")
      - record: ci:step_seconds:regression_threshold
        expr: ci:step_seconds:baseline + clamp_min(0.4 * ci:step_seconds:baseline, 300)
      # the median of each tier's window, given two runs in it; the alert pairs it with the baseline of its own tier
      - record: ci:step_seconds:recent_median
        expr: >-
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[2d]) and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[2d]) >= 2,
            "window", "2d", "", "")
          or
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[14d]) and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[14d]) >= 2
            and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[8d]) >= 1,
            "window", "14d", "", "")
      # the reference it is judged by: the baseline, or the level it was already past its threshold at a day ago
      - record: ci:step_seconds:reference
        expr: >-
          (ci:step_seconds:recent_median offset 1d > ci:step_seconds:regression_threshold offset 1d)
          or ignoring (window) ci:step_seconds:baseline
      - record: ci:step_seconds:reference_threshold
        expr: ci:step_seconds:reference + clamp_min(0.4 * ci:step_seconds:reference, 300)

      # run_wall: the two-day tier where the week before holds three runs, else the two-week tier (`or ignoring (window)`).
      - record: ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline
        expr: >-
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[7d] offset 2d)
            and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[7d] offset 2d) >= 3,
            "window", "2d", "", "")
          or ignoring (window)
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[42d] offset 14d)
            and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[42d] offset 14d) >= 3,
            "window", "14d", "", "")
      - record: ci:run_wall_seconds:regression_threshold
        expr: ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline + clamp_min(0.4 * ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline, 300)
      # the median of each tier's window, given two runs in it; the alert pairs it with the baseline of its own tier
      - record: ci:run_wall_seconds:recent_median
        expr: >-
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[2d]) and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[2d]) >= 2,
            "window", "2d", "", "")
          or
          label_replace(
            quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[14d]) and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[14d]) >= 2
            and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[8d]) >= 1,
            "window", "14d", "", "")
      # the reference it is judged by: the baseline, or the level it was already past its threshold at a day ago
      - record: ci:run_wall_seconds:reference
        expr: >-
          (ci:run_wall_seconds:recent_median offset 1d > ci:run_wall_seconds:regression_threshold offset 1d)
          or ignoring (window) ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline
      - record: ci:run_wall_seconds:reference_threshold
        expr: ci:run_wall_seconds:reference + clamp_min(0.4 * ci:run_wall_seconds:reference, 300)

      # A WHOLE RUN, first job start to last job end: Main's 28 -> 42 minutes.
      - alert: CiRunWallClockRegressed
        expr: >-
          ci:run_wall_seconds:recent_median > ci:run_wall_seconds:reference_threshold
        # HELD SIX HOURS PAST THE LAST EVALUATION THAT SHOWED IT, so a median hovering at the threshold is one episode.
        keep_firing_for: 6h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.workflow }} on main got slower: a median of {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} end to end"
          description: >-
            {{ $recent := "two days" }}{{ $before := "the week" }}{{ $span := "9d" }}{{ $step := "5m" }}{{ $spanText := "9 days" }}{{ if eq $labels.window "14d" }}{{ $recent = "two weeks" }}{{ $before = "the six weeks" }}{{ $span = "56d" }}{{ $step = "1h" }}{{ $spanText = "56 days" }}{{ end }}The successful main runs of {{ $labels.workflow }} over the last {{ $recent }} took a median of
            {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} from their first job's start to their last job's end, against
            {{ $baseline := 0.0 }}{{ with printf `ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline{window=%q,workflow=%q,ref=%q,runner=%q}` $labels.window $labels.workflow $labels.ref $labels.runner | query }}{{ $baseline = . | first | value }}{{ end }}{{ with printf `ci:run_wall_seconds:reference{window=%q,workflow=%q,ref=%q,runner=%q}` $labels.window $labels.workflow $labels.ref $labels.runner | query }}{{ $reference := . | first | value }}{{ if eq $reference $baseline }}a median of {{ $reference | humanizeDuration }} over {{ $before }} before the last {{ $recent }}{{ else }}{{ $reference | humanizeDuration }}, its median a day before, when it was already past its baseline of {{ $baseline | humanizeDuration }}: only the rise since then is new{{ end }}{{ end }}.
            {{ with printf `max_over_time((max without (runner) (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[%s])) and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[%s]) <= ignoring (window) ci:run_wall_seconds:reference_threshold{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,measure="wall",conclusion="success",window=%q} @ end()))[%s:%s])` $labels.workflow $step $labels.workflow $labels.runner $step $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.window $span $step | query }}{{ $good := . | first | value }}Last run at baseline #{{ printf "%.0f" $good }}{{ with printf `min_over_time((max without (runner) (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[%s])) > %f and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[%s]) > ignoring (window) ci:run_wall_seconds:reference_threshold{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,measure="wall",conclusion="success",window=%q} @ end()))[%s:%s])` $labels.workflow $step $good $labels.workflow $labels.runner $step $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.window $span $step | query }}, first slow run after it #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ end }}: the regression is in the commits between them.{{ else }}No run in {{ $spanText }} was at baseline.{{ end }}
            Jobs regressed in it (their own alerts are inhibited by this one):
            {{ range printf `ALERTS{alertname="CiJobDurationRegressed",alertstate="firing",workflow=%q,runner=%q}` $labels.workflow $labels.runner | query }}{{ .Labels.ci_job }}; {{ else }}none.{{ end }}
            Compare the two runs' jobs on the "CI — build timings" dashboard.

      # A JOB: the convergence `takes` rows, a capture leg, a CI shard.
      - alert: CiJobDurationRegressed
        expr: >-
          ci:job_seconds:recent_median > ci:job_seconds:reference_threshold
        # HELD SIX HOURS PAST THE LAST EVALUATION THAT SHOWED IT, so a median hovering at the threshold is one episode.
        keep_firing_for: 6h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.workflow }} / {{ $labels.ci_job }} on main got slower: a median of {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"
          description: >-
            {{ $recent := "two days" }}{{ $before := "the week" }}{{ $span := "9d" }}{{ $step := "5m" }}{{ $spanText := "9 days" }}{{ if eq $labels.window "14d" }}{{ $recent = "two weeks" }}{{ $before = "the six weeks" }}{{ $span = "56d" }}{{ $step = "1h" }}{{ $spanText = "56 days" }}{{ end }}The successful main runs of {{ $labels.ci_job }} ({{ $labels.workflow }}) over the last {{ $recent }} took a
            median of {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}, against
            {{ $baseline := 0.0 }}{{ with printf `ci:job_seconds:baseline{window=%q,workflow=%q,ref=%q,runner=%q,ci_job=%q}` $labels.window $labels.workflow $labels.ref $labels.runner $labels.ci_job | query }}{{ $baseline = . | first | value }}{{ end }}{{ with printf `ci:job_seconds:reference{window=%q,workflow=%q,ref=%q,runner=%q,ci_job=%q}` $labels.window $labels.workflow $labels.ref $labels.runner $labels.ci_job | query }}{{ $reference := . | first | value }}{{ if eq $reference $baseline }}a median of {{ $reference | humanizeDuration }} over {{ $before }} before the last {{ $recent }}{{ else }}{{ $reference | humanizeDuration }}, its median a day before, when it was already past its baseline of {{ $baseline | humanizeDuration }}: only the rise since then is new{{ end }}{{ end }}.
            {{ with printf `max_over_time((max without (runner) (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[%s])) and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,conclusion="success",ci_job=%q}[%s]) <= ignoring (window) ci:job_seconds:reference_threshold{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,conclusion="success",ci_job=%q,window=%q} @ end()))[%s:%s])` $labels.workflow $step $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $step $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $labels.window $span $step | query }}{{ $good := . | first | value }}Last run at baseline #{{ printf "%.0f" $good }}{{ with printf `min_over_time((max without (runner) (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[%s])) > %f and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,conclusion="success",ci_job=%q}[%s]) > ignoring (window) ci:job_seconds:reference_threshold{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,conclusion="success",ci_job=%q,window=%q} @ end()))[%s:%s])` $labels.workflow $step $good $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $step $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $labels.window $span $step | query }}, first slow run after it #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ end }}: the regression is in the commits between them.{{ else }}No run in {{ $spanText }} was at baseline.{{ end }}
            Steps regressed in it (their own alerts are inhibited by this one):
            {{ range printf `ALERTS{alertname="CiStepDurationRegressed",alertstate="firing",workflow=%q,runner=%q,ci_job=%q}` $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.ci_job | query }}{{ .Labels.step }}; {{ else }}none.{{ end }}
            Every job of a workflow slowing at once is the runner host, not a commit. A job that times out has no
            successful run to judge: CiJobNearTimeout is the alert for that.

      # A STEP, which says what inside the job grew. Every step is a candidate; the 5-minute floor keeps the setup and
      # upload steps out unless one really grows.
      - alert: CiStepDurationRegressed
        expr: >-
          ci:step_seconds:recent_median > ci:step_seconds:reference_threshold
        # HELD SIX HOURS PAST THE LAST EVALUATION THAT SHOWED IT, so a median hovering at the threshold is one episode.
        keep_firing_for: 6h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.workflow }} / {{ $labels.ci_job }} step `{{ $labels.step }}` on main got slower: a median of {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"
          description: >-
            {{ $recent := "two days" }}{{ $before := "the week" }}{{ $span := "9d" }}{{ $step := "5m" }}{{ $spanText := "9 days" }}{{ if eq $labels.window "14d" }}{{ $recent = "two weeks" }}{{ $before = "the six weeks" }}{{ $span = "56d" }}{{ $step = "1h" }}{{ $spanText = "56 days" }}{{ end }}The main runs of the step `{{ $labels.step }}` in {{ $labels.ci_job }} ({{ $labels.workflow }}) over the last
            {{ $recent }} took a median of {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}, against
            {{ $baseline := 0.0 }}{{ with printf `ci:step_seconds:baseline{window=%q,workflow=%q,ref=%q,runner=%q,ci_job=%q,step=%q}` $labels.window $labels.workflow $labels.ref $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $labels.step | query }}{{ $baseline = . | first | value }}{{ end }}{{ with printf `ci:step_seconds:reference{window=%q,workflow=%q,ref=%q,runner=%q,ci_job=%q,step=%q}` $labels.window $labels.workflow $labels.ref $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $labels.step | query }}{{ $reference := . | first | value }}{{ if eq $reference $baseline }}a median of {{ $reference | humanizeDuration }} over {{ $before }} before the last {{ $recent }}{{ else }}{{ $reference | humanizeDuration }}, its median a day before, when it was already past its baseline of {{ $baseline | humanizeDuration }}: only the rise since then is new{{ end }}{{ end }}.
            {{ with printf `max_over_time((max without (runner) (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[%s])) and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,ci_job=%q,step=%q}[%s]) <= ignoring (window) ci:step_seconds:reference_threshold{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,ci_job=%q,step=%q,window=%q} @ end()))[%s:%s])` $labels.workflow $step $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $labels.step $step $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $labels.step $labels.window $span $step | query }}{{ $good := . | first | value }}Last run at baseline #{{ printf "%.0f" $good }}{{ with printf `min_over_time((max without (runner) (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[%s])) > %f and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,ci_job=%q,step=%q}[%s]) > ignoring (window) ci:step_seconds:reference_threshold{workflow=%q,ref="main",runner=%q,ci_job=%q,step=%q,window=%q} @ end()))[%s:%s])` $labels.workflow $step $good $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $labels.step $step $labels.workflow $labels.runner $labels.ci_job $labels.step $labels.window $span $step | query }}, first slow run after it #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ end }}: the regression is in the commits between them.{{ else }}No run in {{ $spanText }} was at baseline.{{ end }}

      # WITHIN 80% OF ITS CEILING, whatever the job's conclusion: a job that hit its `timeout-minutes` is cancelled or
      # failed, and that is the run this has to see -- before it, ideally, as US takes at 46.5 of 55 would have been.
      # Main's jobs, and the identity landing checks' (Identity model convergence on measure/ branches), whose takes
      # rows are the ones that timed out; other branch runs are experiments, slow on purpose as often as not.
      # The latest run only: a job's samples of every conclusion, newest first, so a later run back under it clears it.
      - alert: CiJobNearTimeout
        expr: >-
          last_over_time(max without (conclusion) (
              last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main"}[1m])
              or last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="branch",workflow="Identity model convergence"}[1m])
            )[1d:1m])
          >= 0.8 * last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_timeout_seconds[1d])
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.workflow }} / {{ $labels.ci_job }} ({{ $labels.ref }}) took {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}, within 80% of its timeout"
          description: >-
            {{ $labels.ci_job }} ({{ $labels.workflow }}, {{ $labels.ref }}) took {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} in
            {{ with printf `max without (runner) (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref=%q}[1d]))` $labels.workflow $labels.ref | query }}run #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ else }}its latest run{{ end }},
            against a `timeout-minutes` of
            {{ with printf `last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_timeout_seconds{workflow=%q,ref=%q,runner=%q,ci_job=%q}[1d])` $labels.workflow $labels.ref $labels.runner $labels.ci_job | query }}{{ . | first | value | humanizeDuration }}{{ end }}.
            The next slowdown times it out and turns the run red. Find what grew (CiJobDurationRegressed, or the job's
            steps and phases on the "CI — build timings" dashboard) before raising the ceiling: a leg near its limit is
            a signal to re-measure, not to raise the number again (country-convergence-leg.yml).

      # MAIN'S FIRST JOB WAITING FOR A RUNNER, not for the run ahead of it (Main runs one at a time; ci_timings.py
      # `ready_at`). The `main-lane` tier is Main's alone and the target is under 2 minutes: over 5 on two runs in six
      # hours, or 15 once, is the tier short of runners or a runner down.
      - alert: MainCiRunnerWaitHigh
        expr: >-
          (
            last_over_time(max without (conclusion) (
                last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow="Main",ref="main",measure="runner_wait"}[1m])
              )[6h:1m]) > 300
            and
            count_over_time((max without (conclusion) (
                last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow="Main",ref="main",measure="runner_wait"}[1m])
              ) > 300)[6h:1m]) >= 2
          )
          or
          last_over_time(max without (conclusion) (
              last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow="Main",ref="main",measure="runner_wait"}[1m])
            )[6h:1m]) > 900
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "Main's first job waited {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} for a runner"
          description: >-
            {{ with query `max without (runner) (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow="Main",ref="main"}[6h]))` }}Run #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ else }}Main's latest run{{ end }}
            waited {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} from when it could start to its first job starting, against a
            target of under 2 minutes. Check the `main-lane` runners on ci-runner (infra nix/hosts/ci-runner/ci-tiers.nix): one
            down, or a job from another workflow holding the label.
